#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "e2f973d4-5d1e-5e35-84d3-277d1069d6f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12873" :question/problem "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5)$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n10 \\div 5^{0} + (9 - 5) = 10 \\div 5^{0} + 4\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $5^0=1$. So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\div 5^{0} + 4 &=  \\\\\n10 \\div 1 + 4 &=  \\\\\n10 + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97e3c93e-ceff-5bdb-905f-0c245aa0d362" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Negative Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "6f8c20ab-5dcb-5cb2-bf0f-8bc14e019e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-12874" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}$?" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that\n\n$$\n(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot (-2)^{-2} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "222a5d13-74e9-5f1b-81d5-f38a6b80870c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Both Zero and Negative Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "c6b6a911-843f-5c96-a792-ebd807b5d60e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12875" :question/problem "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left(1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 + (-4)^{-2} \\div (1.5 + \\frac{2}{15})^{0} &=  \\\\\n2 + \\frac{1}{16} \\div 1 &=  \\\\\n2 + \\frac{1}{16} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{32}{16} + \\frac{1}{16} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
